# Suburban-SOC — persistent host live capture (SOP-001-C).
# Captures the host interface with native tcpdump (correct net namespace) and pipes the
# pcap stream into a containerized Zeek (-r -) with the threat-intel feed + scan-detection
# policy loaded, writing JSON logs to /storage/PCAP/zeek_logs (tailed by Filebeat).
# systemd keeps it alive across crashes/reboots — closing the recurrence gap that left
# ingest lag unbounded after the Jun-2026 host restart.
#
# IMPORTANT (Docker Desktop / WSL): do NOT capture with `docker run --network host -i eth0`.
# Under Docker Desktop, --network host is the Docker Desktop *utility VM's* namespace
# (192.168.65.0/24), NOT the WSL distro's eth0 — so Zeek would see only Docker-internal
# traffic and miss all real host/LAN traffic (and every sim). Capturing with host-native
# tcpdump and feeding Zeek over stdin is the only approach that sees the real interface
# (this is exactly what scripts/setup/stream_capture.sh raw does).
#
# Install:
#   sudo docker pull zeek/zeek:8.2.1@sha256:eca2b3915d3e067cbb4a904f23f4c4f461ea2b60613ab30f7ee77bbc707c87c7
#   sudo cp configs/systemd/zeek-host-capture.service /etc/systemd/system/
#   sudo systemctl daemon-reload
#   sudo systemctl enable --now zeek-host-capture
# (the explicit pull avoids a fresh host's first start silently retrying forever inside
# Restart=always/StartLimitIntervalSec=0 while systemd reports "activating" the whole time —
# see the #293 note below.)
# Change interface:
#   echo 'CAPTURE_IFACE=ethX' | sudo tee /etc/default/zeek-host-capture && sudo systemctl restart zeek-host-capture
#
# #293: the Zeek container image below is pinned to a specific tag+digest (not
# `:latest`, and not a bare tag), here and in the other 3 real capture paths
# (stream_capture.sh, zeek_connect_host.sh, zeek_run_pcap.sh). Two distinct risks a tag
# alone doesn't close (security-auditor review): (1) an upstream rebuild can silently
# change an OpenSSL/Zeek-generated string value a Sigma rule depends on — already
# happened once, net_zeek_ssl_self_signed_c2.yml's first draft assumed OpenSSL's older
# "self signed certificate" (space) wording, real OpenSSL 3.0.13 in the image emitted
# "self-signed certificate" (hyphenated), caught only by #228's review, not by anything
# that would have failed loudly; (2) a Docker Hub tag is a mutable pointer the publisher
# can re-push, and separately anyone with local Docker socket access can retag an
# arbitrary image as zeek/zeek:8.2.1 — only the content-addressed digest closes both.
# tests/pipeline/test_zeek_image_pin.py enforces all 4 references stay identical and
# match the exact reviewed tag+digest (EXPECTED_TAG/EXPECTED_DIGEST in that file).
# To bump deliberately (#364 security-auditor review — step 2/3 rewritten after that
# review found the original wording named a check that doesn't actually cover the risk):
#   1. Pull the new tag, confirm `zeek --version`, and record its digest
#      (`docker inspect <tag> --format '{{index .RepoDigests 0}}'`).
#   2. The failure class isn't limited to OpenSSL/validation_status — ANY value or
#      schema element the image generates can move under an upstream rebuild (already
#      happened once for real: the OpenSSL wording change above. #228's addition of
#      application/x-pie-executable was ORIGINALLY cited here as a second such case —
#      #365's live verification disproved that: Zeek never emitted that MIME string on
#      ANY version tested, so #228 wasn't reacting to a value that moved, it was
#      reacting to a false premise from the start — application/x-sharedlib already
#      covered the same PIE-executable payloads #228 was testing against, the whole
#      time. Left as a cautionary example of a DIFFERENT risk this same golden-output-
#      diff discipline also catches: a fix based on general-purpose libmagic behavior
#      that doesn't hold for Zeek's own narrower, independent detector). Grepping
#      for one field name (e.g. "validation_status") is not sufficient — it missed,
#      among others, SSH::Password_Guessing (net_zeek_ssh_bruteforce.yml, upstream
#      policy/protocols/ssh/detect-bruteforcing.zeek), Intel::ADDR/Intel::DOMAIN
#      (configs/logstash.conf's SOAR-containment routing), and files.log mime_type/
#      source (net_zeek_executable_download.yml, net_zeek_smtp_attachment_executable.yml).
#      Verify with a golden-output diff instead: run the same fixture PCAP(s) through
#      the old and new image with the real production @load chain
#      (config.zeek + scan-detection.zeek + detect-bruteforcing), diff the resulting
#      logs' key sets and the specific values rule logic depends on. `zeek -e 'print
#      SSH::Password_Guessing; print Intel::ADDR'` (loading the relevant policy first)
#      is a fast spot-check for enum-shaped dependencies specifically.
#   3. tests/detections/test_live_fire.py does NOT invoke Zeek — it indexes repo-
#      authored fixtures straight into Elasticsearch, so it passes identically
#      regardless of which Zeek image (or none) is installed. It's still worth
#      re-running (confirms the ES/Sigma half didn't regress), but it is not evidence
#      about the image itself — only the golden-output diff in step 2 is.
#   4. Update all 4 real capture paths together, plus EXPECTED_TAG/EXPECTED_DIGEST in
#      tests/pipeline/test_zeek_image_pin.py, plus the example in
#      docs/Zeek_ELK_Pipeline.md (not a real capture path, not covered by
#      test_zeek_image_pin.py, but a tracked sync target since #293 — a prior bump
#      missed it). docs/SOP-147-evidence-validation-procedure.md's evidence-collection
#      commands filter on `--filter name=zeek-` (#364), not the image tag, so they no
#      longer need touching on future bumps.
#   5. Re-run test_zeek_image_pin.py.
#
# #364: bumped 8.1.1 -> 8.2.1 (OpenSSL 3.0.13 -> 3.5.6) to close 7 real CRITICAL CVEs
# #355's new Trivy job found on 8.1.1 (libgnutls30t64, libssl3t64/openssl, libnode115/
# nodejs) — 8.2.1 scans clean. Verification actually performed (per the rewritten
# checklist above, not just the original narrower step 2/3 wording):
#   - Both validation_status-dependent rules (net_zeek_ssl_self_signed_c2.yml,
#     net_zeek_ssl_expired_cert_connection.yml) re-verified against the real new
#     OpenSSL via `openssl verify` on freshly-generated self-signed/expired certs
#     inside the new image — both exact strings ("self-signed certificate",
#     "certificate has expired") are byte-identical to the old OpenSSL. Confirmed
#     `zeek` itself dynamically links the SAME libssl.so.3/libcrypto.so.3 the CLI
#     check used (`ldd $(command -v zeek)` inside the image), not just an assumed proxy.
#   - SSH::Password_Guessing/SSH::Login_By_Password_Guesser and Intel::ADDR/
#     Intel::DOMAIN confirmed to still exist under the new image via `zeek -e 'print'`.
#   - files.log mime_type/source re-verified with real (not fixture-only) traffic: a
#     synthetic-but-real HTTP download of an ELF binary and a shell script, each run
#     through OLD and NEW images and diffed — byte-identical mime_type/source on both
#     ("application/x-sharedlib"/"HTTP" for the ELF; "text/x-shellscript"/"HTTP" for
#     the script — the latter is a PRE-EXISTING gap versus the rule's own expected
#     list, unrelated to this bump, confirmed identical on both versions; tracked
#     separately).
#   - All repo-depended-on @load paths (policy/frameworks/intel/{seen,do_notice},
#     policy/protocols/ssl/validate-certs, the #288 capture-loss monitoring policy,
#     policy/protocols/conn/mac-logging, policy/protocols/ssh/detect-bruteforcing,
#     base/frameworks/notice) syntax-checked clean against the new image.
#   - Real offline-PCAP-replay and streaming-pattern invocations against the new
#     image, output compared directly against the same run on the old image —
#     identical conn.log/reporter.log shape, no new errors.
#   - All 18 tests/detections/test_live_fire.py tests pass against a real
#     Elasticsearch cluster (confirms the ES/Sigma half; see step 3 above for why
#     this alone isn't evidence about the image).
#   - Found live during this bump (security-auditor): docs/SOP-147-evidence-
#     validation-procedure.md's `ancestor=zeek/zeek:<tag>` filter is unreliable — a
#     container started via `repo:tag@digest` does not reliably get that tag applied
#     to the local image store (confirmed empirically: a fresh `docker pull`+`docker
#     run` of the new pin left `docker ps --filter ancestor=zeek/zeek:8.2.1` matching
#     nothing despite the exact right container running). Fixed by switching to
#     `--filter name=zeek-` (stream_capture.sh now sets --name zeek-stream, matching
#     this unit's existing --name zeek-host-capture) — deterministic regardless of
#     Docker's tag-caching behavior or which tag is currently pinned.
[Unit]
Description=Suburban-SOC Zeek host live capture (tcpdump -> Zeek; iface from CAPTURE_IFACE, default eth0)
Documentation=https://github.com/voltron-1/Suburban-SOC
# Under Docker Desktop + WSL there is NO host docker.service systemd unit, so we do not
# Requires=/After= it; we order after the network and let Restart=always wait for the
# Docker engine (Docker Desktop) to become reachable after boot.
After=network-online.target
Wants=network-online.target
# Don't let the restart rate-limiter give up while waiting for the Docker engine.
StartLimitIntervalSec=0

[Service]
Type=simple
# CAP_CHOWN: the ExecStartPre steps below (chgrp .../intel to tjlam, and
# chown .../intel.dat to tjlam) need it — this unit runs as root but
# CapabilityBoundingSet= still gates what root itself can do, so a bare
# UID 0 is not enough. Missing here since #209 added this line before #222
# later added a chown step on top of it: reproduced live on this exact host
# (systemctl status showed that step failing "Operation not permitted", not
# a filesystem/ownership issue — the classic EPERM signature of a missing
# capability, not EACCES). No setfacl/getfacl available on this host —
# otherwise an ACL grant (adds tjlam access without transferring root's own)
# would have avoided needing this capability entirely (security-auditor
# review).
# CAP_SETUID/CAP_SETGID: found live on this exact host AFTER the CAP_CHOWN
# fix above was already deployed and working (two clean restarts) — a THIRD
# restart crash-looped with "tcpdump: Couldn't change to 'tcpdump'
# uid=105 gid=110: Operation not permitted". Per `man tcpdump`: "If run as
# root ... tcpdump will try to change the tcpdump process's ... user ID to
# that of ... tcpdump ... This behavior is enabled by DEFAULT (-Z tcpdump)."
# I.e. tcpdump has always tried this on this host (the dedicated `tcpdump`
# system user already existed) — the EPERM is deterministic given the
# bounding set, not host-state flakiness (security-auditor emergency
# review): the two restarts that didn't hit it either ran against a stale
# /etc/systemd/system/ copy that predated this bounding set entirely, or
# their "success" was only ever conn.log traffic from a DIFFERENT capture
# path (scripts/setup/stream_capture.sh, an unrestricted-sudo session that
# does not need these capabilities) — at the time, ExecStart below had no
# `pipefail`, so a dead tcpdump leg would not have failed the unit as long
# as the docker leg was still up. Fixed by adding `set -o pipefail` below
# so this class of masked failure surfaces going forward (it no longer
# applies to the ExecStart as it stands now). This is the CORRECT fix, not a workaround:
# tcpdump shedding root for an unprivileged, purpose-built system user after
# opening the raw socket is exactly the kind of privilege minimization this
# whole capability-narrowing effort (#182/#209) is for — the alternative
# (`-Z root` on the ExecStart line, disabling the drop) would leave tcpdump
# running as root for its entire lifetime instead.
CapabilityBoundingSet=CAP_NET_RAW CAP_NET_ADMIN CAP_CHOWN CAP_SETUID CAP_SETGID
# Default interface; override via /etc/default/zeek-host-capture (optional).
Environment=CAPTURE_IFACE=eth0
# #320: default deployment user/repo path, referenced below as ${SOC_USER}/
# ${SOC_REPO} instead of hardcoding tjlam/this absolute path in every
# ExecStartPre/ExecStart line — this unit hard-fails on any host without
# that exact user/path otherwise. Override via the same env file below for
# a different deployment. Same substitution mechanism as CAPTURE_IFACE
# above (systemd expands ${VAR} into the command string before bash parses
# it): the ExecStartPre lines below still interpolate these directly rather
# than taking them as a script's positional argument the way host_capture.sh
# now does for CAPTURE_IFACE (#320 finding 2) — accepted here since writing
# this env file already requires root (same low-severity framing #320's own
# CAPTURE_IFACE finding used), and this specific hardening pass is scoped to
# portability, not re-plumbing every ExecStartPre in this already-fragile
# unit (see the reverted-sandboxing note in this file's [Service] footer)
# into a script of its own.
Environment=SOC_USER=tjlam
Environment=SOC_REPO=/home/tjlam/projects/Suburban-SOC
EnvironmentFile=-/etc/default/zeek-host-capture
# CWE-59 (security-auditor emergency review, HIGH, pre-existing): /storage/PCAP
# is tjlam-owned, so a tjlam-level actor can replace /storage/PCAP/intel (or
# zeek_logs) with a symlink (e.g. to /etc/sudoers.d) before a restart. mkdir -p
# and cp -r both follow symlinks silently, so root's own cp below would then
# write every file under configs/intel/* into the symlink target as
# root:root — full tjlam->root escalation, and neither the chown/chmod -h
# guards below cover it since those run after mkdir/cp already succeeded.
# security-auditor re-review: checking the directory itself is NOT enough —
# intel.dat is deliberately tjlam-OWNED (see the chown below), and the sticky
# bit permits an owner to unlink/replace its own file, so tjlam can swap
# intel.dat itself for a symlink without touching the directory at all. Sweep
# for symlinks one level down too, not just the directory entries themselves.
# Fail closed instead of following any of it.
ExecStartPre=/bin/bash -c '[ ! -L /storage/PCAP/intel ] && [ ! -L /storage/PCAP/zeek_logs ] || { echo "FATAL: /storage/PCAP/intel or zeek_logs is a symlink, refusing to follow it" >&2; exit 1; }; [ -z "$(find /storage/PCAP/intel -maxdepth 1 -type l -print -quit 2>/dev/null)" ] || { echo "FATAL: symlink found inside /storage/PCAP/intel, refusing to follow it" >&2; exit 1; }'
# Refresh threat-intel configs into the host volume Zeek reads (mirrors the scripts).
ExecStartPre=/bin/mkdir -p /storage/PCAP/zeek_logs /storage/PCAP/intel
# #222: config.zeek's zeek_init() calls suspend_processing() and only resumes
# on Input::end_of_data for /data/intel/intel.dat (config.zeek:33-41) - if that
# file does not exist AT ALL when Zeek starts, that event never fires and
# packet processing hangs forever (reproduced directly: `zeek -r - config.zeek`
# with no intel.dat present logs "processing suspended" / a read error and
# never resumes). configs/intel/intel.dat is now gitignored (#222 - it's
# fully regenerated by intel-refresh.timer, keeping it tracked meant every 6h
# refresh left a permanent uncommitted diff) — so a fresh checkout, or any
# host where intel-refresh.timer has not fired even once yet, has no intel.dat
# for the `cp -r` below to pick up. The fallback line guarantees SOMETHING
# valid always exists before Zeek starts: intel.seed.dat (always tracked,
# deterministic) copied in as intel.dat if intel-refresh.timer has not
# produced a live-merged one yet. The timer's first run (5 min after boot,
# see intel-refresh.timer) overwrites this with the real merged feed shortly
# after, via refresh_intel.sh's own LIVE_DIR sync step.
# security-auditor review (2nd pass): an earlier draft of this fix instead
# did `chown tjlam:tjlam` (transferring ownership away from root) and moved
# it to run AFTER this cp step specifically so root could still write here
# on THIS run — but that only holds for the very first start ever. Once the
# directory is tjlam-owned, EVERY later restart hits this cp BEFORE any
# chown step runs again, and root has already permanently lost write access
# from the previous start — turning the repo->sensor policy sync into a
# silent, permanent no-op from the second restart onward, invisible because
# of the `2>/dev/null || true` below. The chgrp+sticky-bit design below
# fixes this at the root (root never stops owning the directory), so this
# cp keeps working on every single restart, not just the first.
# security-auditor review (3rd pass): "-s" (non-empty), not "-f" (merely
# exists) — a zero-byte/truncated intel.dat would otherwise pass this check
# and skip the seed fallback, and per config.zeek:33-41 an intel.dat that
# never reaches Input::end_of_data leaves packet processing permanently
# suspended while systemd still reports the unit "active (running)".
# "--remove-destination": defense-in-depth for the TOCTOU window between the
# symlink sweep above (a separate ExecStartPre process) and this cp — GNU cp
# without it opens an *existing* destination symlink's target for writing
# (follows it) rather than replacing the link; --remove-destination unlinks
# the destination path first so a symlink planted in that window is deleted,
# not written through, and root owns this directory so the unlink succeeds
# regardless of who owns the (removed) symlink (security-auditor re-review).
# #288: the intel.dat check above only guards intel.dat specifically —
# config.zeek itself had no equivalent verification, so a silently-failed
# cp left Zeek running whatever stale config.zeek was already on disk (with
# none of the repo's own @load list, e.g. this same fix's new capture-loss
# line) with ExecStart still reporting a normal, successful start. Checking
# for the newest @load line (capture-loss) rather than an older one like
# validate-certs, which could already exist in a stale copy from before
# this fix. A failure here fails ExecStartPre, which blocks ExecStart —
# the loud failure this finding asked for, not a silent stale-config start.
# security-auditor review: appending this check made it the LAST command in
# the bash -c string, and bash -c reports only the last commands own exit
# status — the intel.dat fallback cp just above (also best-effort until
# this point) could now fail its own way and be silently swallowed instead
# of blocking ExecStart the way #222 originally guaranteed. set -e at the
# front restores that: the fallback cp is still allowed to fail when
# intel.dat already exists (the [ -s ... ] check short-circuits it, an
# already-successful || chain set -e does not touch), but a REAL fallback
# failure (intel.dat absent AND the seed cp itself fails) now aborts here
# too, same as before this fix.
# #321: --remove-destination on the intel.dat fallback cp too (the config.zeek
# cp above it already has it) — intel.dat is deliberately tjlam-OWNED, not
# merely group-writable (see the chown below), so its own sticky-bit
# protection doesn't apply to itself: tjlam can unlink intel.dat, plant a
# dangling symlink (e.g. to /etc/cron.d/pwn), and the next restart's fallback
# cp (triggered because the dangling link fails [ -s ]) would otherwise write
# configs/intel/intel.seed.dat's content THROUGH that symlink as root.
# --remove-destination unlinks the destination path first instead, so a
# planted symlink is deleted, not written through — same reasoning as the
# config.zeek cp's own --remove-destination above.
ExecStartPre=/bin/bash -c 'set -e; cp -r --remove-destination ${SOC_REPO}/configs/intel/* /storage/PCAP/intel/ 2>/dev/null || true; [ -s /storage/PCAP/intel/intel.dat ] || cp --remove-destination ${SOC_REPO}/configs/intel/intel.seed.dat /storage/PCAP/intel/intel.dat; grep -q "policy/misc/capture-loss" /storage/PCAP/intel/config.zeek || { echo "FATAL: deployed config.zeek is missing an expected at-load line - stale copy" >&2; exit 1; }'
# intel-refresh.service (configs/systemd/intel-refresh.service) runs as the
# unprivileged repo-owner user and needs to WRITE intel.dat into this same
# directory on every scheduled refresh, not just at Zeek's own startup — but
# the mkdir above leaves it root:root. Found live on this exact host while
# building #222: /storage/PCAP/intel was already root:root 755 (an earlier
# start's own doing) while its parent /storage/PCAP is tjlam:tjlam -
# refresh_intel.sh's live-sync `cp` was silently failing every run ("NOTE:
# could not write ... (permissions?)" in its own log) and the running Zeek
# capture was never actually getting the refreshed feed between restarts.
# security-auditor review (2nd pass): ADD tjlam access via group ownership +
# sticky bit rather than TRANSFER ownership via a plain chown (the #222
# original, and this fix's own first draft) — root stays the directory
# OWNER permanently, so it never loses its own write access on any later
# restart (closing the cp-becomes-a-no-op gap above), and the sticky bit
# (mode 1775) means tjlam can create/modify files here (the group-write bit)
# but can only DELETE/rename files it itself owns. This raises the bar for
# tampering with config.zeek in place (stays root-owned) but is NOT a full
# guarantee (security-auditor review, 3rd pass): /storage/PCAP itself is
# tjlam-owned (see the earlier comment above), so a sufficiently motivated
# tjlam-level actor could still rename this whole directory aside and
# substitute one of its own before a restart — sticky-bit protection inside
# a directory does not extend to protecting the directory's own identity.
# security-auditor review (emergency pass): the sticky bit is ALSO not the
# actual barrier to tampering with config.zeek's *content* day-to-day — the
# cp -r above re-installs configs/intel/config.zeek from the tjlam-writable
# repo checkout on every single restart, so anyone who can edit that repo
# file gets it loaded into the root-owned directory (and executed by Zeek
# as the capture container's policy) on the next restart with no sticky-bit
# interaction at all. Closing that (and the directory-rename gap above)
# needs config.zeek served from a root-owned path outside this tree
# entirely; tracked as a follow-up (see #270), not fixed here.
# "-h" on both chown lines: GNU chown dereferences a symlink argument by
# default, so a chown (unlike this chgrp-via-chown, and the intel.dat one
# below) run against a symlinked path would retarget whatever the symlink
# points at instead — "-h" operates on the link itself, a no-op on a real
# directory/file but a real guard if /storage/PCAP/intel or intel.dat were
# ever replaced by a symlink (accidentally or otherwise) before this runs.
# chmod has no "-h"/no-dereference equivalent on Linux (symlinks carry no
# permission bits of their own to change) — a symlinked /storage/PCAP/intel
# would apply this chmod to whatever the link points at instead. Chained
# with the chown via && (not two separate "-"-prefixed lines) so a failed
# chown can't leave a root:root directory silently chmod'd to 1775 by the
# next line running anyway — either both apply, or the whole chain no-ops
# and is retried next restart (security-auditor review, 3rd pass).
# "-"-prefixed: capture availability outranks intel freshness — a failure
# in this self-heal chain must not crash-loop the SOC's only network
# sensor. A failure here degrades to refresh_intel.sh's own tagged "stale"
# status (visible via slo_metrics.py's metric_intel_feed_stale_heartbeats(),
# #358), not a capture outage.
# #321: chown -h above already refuses to dereference a symlink AT
# /storage/PCAP/intel itself, but chmod's total lack of a "-h" equivalent
# (see above) means the real risk window is between these two commands —
# tjlam (sticky-bit write access to the directory, not root) could unlink
# /storage/PCAP/intel and replant it as a symlink to e.g. /etc/cron.d in the
# instant after chown succeeds but before chmod runs, redirecting the chmod
# onto an attacker-controlled target. This hard (non-"-"-prefixed) guard
# runs first and hard-fails ExecStart if the path is missing or is already
# a symlink, narrowing — not eliminating, since the swap could still race
# in between this check and the chown/chmod line below — the window an
# attacker gets to act in. Deliberately NOT "-"-prefixed: unlike the
# self-heal chain below, a symlink found here means the directory is
# already compromised, and starting capture against it would be worse than
# refusing to start (security-auditor review, per issue #321's own
# suggested fix).
ExecStartPre=/bin/bash -c '[ -d /storage/PCAP/intel ] && [ ! -L /storage/PCAP/intel ]'
ExecStartPre=-/bin/bash -c 'chown -h root:${SOC_USER} /storage/PCAP/intel && chmod 1775 /storage/PCAP/intel'
# intel.dat itself (not just the directory) needs to be tjlam-OWNED, not
# merely group-writable: refresh_intel.sh's live sync writes a temp file
# then renames it over intel.dat (configs/intel/refresh_intel.sh) — and the
# sticky bit above only permits a rename-over (an implicit unlink of the
# replaced file) by that file's OWNER or the directory's owner. tjlam is
# neither by default (root owns both), so without this line the sticky bit
# that protects config.zeek would ALSO block the one write intel-refresh
# actually needs to make. config.zeek itself is deliberately left off this
# line — it stays root:root, protected by the sticky bit above.
ExecStartPre=-/bin/chown -h ${SOC_USER}:${SOC_USER} /storage/PCAP/intel/intel.dat
# Clear any stale container left by a hard kill (the run uses --rm for clean exit).
ExecStartPre=-/usr/bin/docker rm -f zeek-host-capture
# tcpdump captures in the host (WSL) net namespace; Zeek parses the live pcap from stdin.
# Runs as root under systemd, so tcpdump needs no separate sudo.
# #320: the tcpdump|docker pipeline itself now lives in scripts/setup/
# host_capture.sh, invoked with CAPTURE_IFACE as a quoted positional
# argument rather than interpolated into this line's own command string —
# see that script's own header comment for the shell-injection gap this
# closes (a hostile /etc/default/zeek-host-capture CAPTURE_IFACE value can
# no longer break out of the tcpdump invocation and run as root). The
# script preserves "set -o pipefail" internally, same reasoning as before:
# without it, a dead/EPERM'd tcpdump leg (e.g. the CAP_SETUID/CAP_SETGID
# crash above) would leave systemd reporting this unit healthy while no
# packets are actually being captured (security-auditor emergency review —
# this is the likely reason the first two restarts read as "clean" before
# the third crash-looped).
ExecStart=/bin/bash ${SOC_REPO}/scripts/setup/host_capture.sh "${CAPTURE_IFACE}"
ExecStop=-/usr/bin/docker stop -t 10 zeek-host-capture
Restart=always
RestartSec=5

# audit #167 / NIST CM-7: a sandboxing directive block was added here and then
# REVERTED (2026-07-09) — it broke live capture in production (crash-loop,
# ExecStart exit 1) on this Docker Desktop/WSL2 host despite being deliberately
# conservative (no capability/User= changes). Root cause not yet isolated; see
# issue #182, which now also covers finding which directive(s) are actually
# safe here via careful one-at-a-time live testing, not a blind reapply.

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
